# USAGE/2024-10-29: If this script fails to run, permiss it.
#
# - Apple menu > System Settings...
#     > Privacy & Security
#       > Accessibility
#         > ✓ [Your Favorite Term. App, e.g., Alacritty | iTerm | Terminal]
#
# BWARE: If this script fails to run from Alacritty, and if Alacritty already
# has Accessibility access, you might need to reset *all* Accessibility
# permissions to recover.
#
# - E.g., if you see the following error:
#
#   $ osascript ~/.kit/sh/sh-humble-prompt/lib/window-title--alacritty-number.osa
#   /Users/user/.kit/sh/sh-humble-prompt/lib/window-title--alacritty-number.osa:2389:2670:
#     execution error: System Events got an error: osascript is not allowed assistive access. (-25211)
#
# and disable/enable Alacritty permissions and rebooting doesn't help, you
# may need to reset all a11y permissions.
#
# - Run the following:
#
#     sudo tccutil reset Accessibility
#
# - And then cold reset, because that command will likely render
#   all mouse and keyboard events nonresponsive.
#
# - Then reboot and reassign Accessibility permissions to all your apps
#   (so maybe take a screenshot of Accessibility settings first, so you
#   have a list of everything to restore).
#
# - REFER: See (Closed) Alacritty issue about this (because the resolution
#   is to call `sudo tccutil reset Accessibility`, silly):
#
#     https://github.com/alacritty/alacritty/issues/7334
#
#   - See also DepoXy macOS ONBRD doc§:
#
#     https://github.com/DepoXy/depoxy/blob/release/docs/README-macOS-onboarding.rst#adhoc-reactivate-alacritty-assistive-access-if-osa-starts-failing

# INERT/2024-07-23: Replace (or Complement) this script with Hammerspoon
# implementation (which could work on any terminal app; might be quicker;
# and would be easier to maintain).

# SAVVY: Alacritty is not scriptable.
#
# - E.g., this fails:
#
#     tell application "Alacritty"
#       ...
#         repeat with w in windows
#
#   Indicating:
#
#     .../lib/window-title--alacritty-number.osa:369:526:
#       execution error: Alacritty got an error:
#         every window doesn’t understand the “count” message. (-1708)
#
# - Fortunately there is an alternative solution:
#
#   "The only way to target a non-scriptable application's windows is via
#    the properties of [application] process objects from the System Events
#    context - not via its application object (only scriptable application
#    objects have windows elements)"
#
#   - REFER:
#     https://stackoverflow.com/questions/33420848/applescript-get-title-of-a-window

# DUNNO/2024-07-23: After testing Hammerspoon for a while tonight,
# this AppleScript stopped detecting the window prefixed "1. "
# (and then any time you opened a new terminal it was numbered 1).
#
# - It appears to do with this alternative tell-Alacritty approach:
#
#     tell application "Alacritty"
#       activate
#     end tell
#
#     tell application "System Events"
#       -- Get the frontmost app's *process* object.
#       set frontAppProcess to first application process whose frontmost is true
#     end tell
#
#     # Tell the *process* to iterate its windows.
#     tell frontAppProcess
#       local numbersList
#       set numbersList to { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 }
#       ...
#
# - But using 'tell processes whose name is "Alacritty"'
#   instead worked, and included the "missing" windows.

tell application "System Events"
  tell processes whose name is "Alacritty"
    local numbersList
    set numbersList to { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 }
    repeat with idx from 1 to length of numbersList
      set currNum to item idx of numbersList
      -- TRACE: Uncomment for trace:
      --  log "Checking no. " & currNum

      -- MAYBE: Maintain list of windows and remove when matches
      --        so we don't iterate through full list every time
      repeat with w in windows
        tell w
          -- TRACE: Uncomment for trace:
          --  log "Title: " & NAME of w

          if NAME of w starts with currNum & ". " then
            set currNum to 0

            exit repeat
          end if
        end tell
      end repeat

      if currNum is not 0
        return currNum
      end if
    end repeat
  end tell
end tell

